## TL;DR  
Yes – you can draw contour lines **on‑the‑fly** from a raster‑DEM source in MapLibre GL JS, but you have to do the heavy lifting yourself (or off‑load it to a worker/GPU). The typical recipe is:

1. **Fetch the DEM tiles** you need (e.g. GeoTIFF, Cloud‑Optimized GeoTIFF, or any raster‑DEM tile service).  
2. **Decode the raster** into a plain elevation grid (Array‑of‑Floats) with a client‑side decoder such as **geotiff.js**.  
3. **Run a contouring algorithm** (marching‑squares / marching‑cubes) on that grid – libraries like **d3‑contour**, **marchingsquares.js**, or **turf.isoline** (after converting the grid to points) do the job.  
4. **Convert the resulting isolines to GeoJSON** (or a `FeatureCollection` of `LineString`s).  
5. **Add the GeoJSON as a source** to MapLibre and style it with a `line` layer.  
6. **Re‑run the pipeline** whenever the viewport changes (move/zoom) – throttled, cached, and preferably done in a Web Worker.

Below is a **step‑by‑step guide**, a **minimal working example**, and a set of **practical tips** to keep the experience smooth.

---

## 1. Why you can’t just “turn on” contours in MapLibre

MapLibre (and its ancestor Mapbox GL JS) knows how to render a **raster‑DEM** for hill‑shading, terrain extrusion, etc., but it does **not** contain a built‑in isoline generator. Contour lines are vector data, so the usual production workflow is:

```
raster DEM  →  GDAL / tippecanoe  →  vector contour tiles  →  MapLibre
```

If you want to avoid the pre‑rendered vector tiles, you must **generate the isolines in the browser** (or in a serverless function that runs on demand). The browser route is what the question asks for.

---

## 2. High‑level architecture

```
+-------------------+          +-------------------+          +-------------------+
|  MapLibre GL JS   |  <--->   |  Web Worker /    |  <--->   |  DEM Tile Service |
| (map, view, UI)   |          |  GPU (optional)  |          | (GeoTIFF, XYZ, …) |
+-------------------+          +-------------------+          +-------------------+
        ^                               |
        |                               v
        |                     +-------------------+
        |                     |  Contour Engine   |
        |                     | (geotiff.js +     |
        |                     |  d3‑contour /     |
        |                     |  marching‑squares)|
        |                     +-------------------+
        |                               |
        +----------- GeoJSON ----------+
```

* **MapLibre** only consumes the final GeoJSON (or vector‑tile) source.  
* **Web Worker** (or a GPU‑accelerated library) does the heavy lifting so the UI thread stays responsive.  
* **DEM Tile Service** can be any raster source that returns tiles in a format you can decode (GeoTIFF is the most flexible).

---

## 3. Required libraries

| Purpose | Library | CDN / npm |
|---------|---------|-----------|
| Decode GeoTIFF/COG tiles | **geotiff.js** | `https://unpkg.com/geotiff@2.1.3/dist-browser/geotiff.min.js` |
| Generate isolines | **d3‑contour** (or **marchingsquares.js**) | `https://unpkg.com/d3-contour@3` |
| (Optional) Off‑load work | **comlink** + **worker-loader** | `https://unpkg.com/comlink@4` |
| (Optional) GPU acceleration | **gpu.js** or **WebGL2 compute** | `https://unpkg.com/gpu.js@2` |
| MapLibre GL JS | **maplibre-gl** | `https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4` |

> **Why d3‑contour?**  
> It implements the classic *marching‑squares* algorithm, works on a simple 2‑D array, and returns GeoJSON‑compatible `MultiPolygon`/`MultiLineString` objects out of the box.

---

## 4. Step‑by‑step implementation

Below is a **complete, minimal example** that you can paste into an HTML file and run locally (assuming you have a COG/GeoTIFF DEM endpoint). Replace the `DEM_URL_TEMPLATE` with a real tile URL (e.g. `https://myserver/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.tif`).

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre DEM → Contours on the fly</title>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4"></script>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/geotiff@2.1.3/dist-browser/geotiff.min.js"></script>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/d3-contour@3"></script>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   1️⃣  MAP INITIALISATION
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // Base map – you can swap this for any raster/vector source you like
      osm: {
        type: 'raster',
        tiles: ['https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
        tileSize: 256,
        attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
      }
    },
    layers: [{ id: 'osm', type: 'raster', source: 'osm' }]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   2️⃣  DEM TILE TEMPLATE
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
// Example: a public Cloud‑Optimized GeoTIFF tile service.
// You must have CORS enabled on the server.
const DEM_URL_TEMPLATE = 'https://my-dem-server/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.tif';

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   3️⃣  CONTOUR SETTINGS
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const contourIntervals = [0, 100, 200, 500, 1000, 1500, 2000]; // metres
const contourColor = '#ff6600';
const contourWidth = 1.2;

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   4️⃣  HELPER: fetch & decode a single DEM tile
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
async function fetchDemTile(z, x, y) {
  const url = DEM_URL_TEMPLATE.replace('{z}', z)
                              .replace('{x}', x)
                              .replace('{y}', y);
  const response = await fetch(url);
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`DEM tile ${z}/${x}/${y} failed`);
  const arrayBuffer = await response.arrayBuffer();
  const tiff = await GeoTIFF.fromArrayBuffer(arrayBuffer);
  const image = await tiff.getImage(); // assumes one image per tile
  const raster = await image.readRasters({ interleave: true });
  const tiepoint = image.getTiePoints()[0];
  const pixelScale = image.getFileDirectory().ModelPixelScale;
  return {
    data: raster,               // Float32Array (or Uint16Array) of elevation values
    width: image.getWidth(),
    height: image.getHeight(),
    tiepoint,
    pixelScale
  };
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   5️⃣  HELPER: convert raster → elevation grid (2‑D array)
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
function rasterToGrid(tile) {
  const { data, width, height } = tile;
  // d3‑contour expects a flat array in row‑major order.
  // Most DEMs are already in that order, so we can just return data.
  // If you need to flip Y, do it here.
  return { values: data, width, height };
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   6️⃣  HELPER: generate isolines for a single tile
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
function generateContours(grid, intervals) {
  const { values, width, height } = grid;
  // d3‑contour works on a *regular* grid. We give it the flat array.
  const contours = d3.contours()
      .size([width, height])
      .thresholds(intervals)(values);
  // Each contour is a GeoJSON MultiPolygon; we’ll convert to LineString later.
  return contours;
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   7️⃣  HELPER: convert d3‑contour MultiPolygons → GeoJSON LineString
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
function polygonsToLines(contourFeature, tile) {
  // d3‑contour returns polygons (outer ring + holes). For visualising
  // as contour *lines* we can just keep the outer rings.
  const { coordinates } = contourFeature;
  const lines = coordinates.map(ring => ({
    type: 'Feature',
    properties: { elevation: contourFeature.value },
    geometry: {
      type: 'LineString',
      coordinates: ring[0].map(([x, y]) => {
        // Convert pixel coordinates back to WebMercator lon/lat.
        const lon = tile.tiepoint.x + x * tile.pixelScale[0];
        const lat = tile.tiepoint.y - y * tile.pixelScale[1];
        // Convert from EPSG:3857 (WebMercator meters) to lon/lat.
        const R = 6378137;
        const λ = (lon / R) * 180 / Math.PI;
        const φ = (2 * Math.atan(Math.exp(lat / R)) - Math.PI / 2) * 180 / Math.PI;
        return [λ, φ];
      })
    }
  }));
  return lines;
}

