Below is a quick‑to‑apply checklist plus a **complete working example** that will turn a “hard‑edge, over‑exposed” hillshade into a smooth, natural‑looking relief surface in MapLibre GL JS.

---

## 1️⃣ Why hillshades look “harsh”

| Reason | What it does | How to tame it |
|--------|--------------|----------------|
| **Exaggeration** (`hillshade-exaggeration`) | Multiplies the slope‑derived shading. The default (`1`) can be too strong for low‑resolution DEMs. | Lower it (e.g. `0.4‑0.7`). |
| **Illumination direction** (`hillshade-illumination-direction`) | Sun azimuth in degrees. A direction that hits many ridges head‑on creates stark black‑white strips. | Rotate it a bit (e.g. `315°` or `45°`). |
| **Shadow / highlight colors** (`hillshade-shadow-color`, `hillshade-highlight-color`) | Pure black / pure white are the defaults. | Use semi‑transparent greys (`rgba(0,0,0,0.5)`, `rgba(255,255,255,0.4)`). |
| **Opacity** (`raster-opacity` on the hillshade layer) | 100 % opacity makes the hillshade dominate the map. | Drop to `0.6‑0.8`. |
| **DEM resolution / zoom** | Low‑res DEMs produce blocky slopes that look “pixelated”. | Use a higher‑zoom DEM source or limit the hillshade to zoom levels where the DEM is detailed enough. |
| **Ambient occlusion** (`hillshade-ambient-occlusion-intensity`) | Adds subtle “soft shadows” in valleys. | Turn it on (`0.5‑0.8`) for a more natural feel. |

---

## 2️⃣ Minimal working example

> **TL;DR** – copy‑paste this into a blank HTML file, replace the DEM URL with your own (or use the public MapLibre DEM), and you’ll see a nicely‑toned hillshade.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre GL JS – Soft Hillshade</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no" />
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Base map – you can swap this for any raster/vector source you like
        "osm": {
          type: "raster",
          tiles: ["https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"],
          tileSize: 256,
          attribution: "© OpenStreetMap contributors"
        },

        // 2️⃣ DEM source (raster-dem) – replace with your own if you have one
        "dem": {
          type: "raster-dem",
          // Public DEM from MapLibre (SRTM‑30m, global)
          url: "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain-tiles/tiles.json",
          tileSize: 256,
          // Important: tell the renderer the DEM is in EPSG:3857 (WebMercator)
          // (most DEM tiles are already in that projection)
          // If you use a custom source, set `maxzoom` to the highest zoom you have.
          maxzoom: 12
        }
      },
      layers: [
        // Base raster basemap
        {
          id: "osm-basemap",
          type: "raster",
          source: "osm"
        },

        // 3️⃣ Hillshade layer – this is where the magic happens
        {
          id: "hillshade",
          type: "hillshade",
          source: "dem",
          paint: {
            // ---- Softening the look -------------------------------------------------
            // Reduce the contrast
            "hillshade-exaggeration": 0.55,               // 0‑1 is usually enough
            // Rotate the sun a little so shadows aren’t all straight north‑south
            "hillshade-illumination-direction": 315,      // degrees clockwise from north
            // Keep the sun overhead but not too high (optional)
            // "hillshade-illumination-anchor": "viewport", // default

            // Use semi‑transparent colours instead of pure black/white
            "hillshade-shadow-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.45)",
            "hillshade-highlight-color": "rgba(255,255,255,0.35)",

            // Add a gentle ambient‑occlusion term (soft valley shadows)
            "hillshade-ambient-occlusion-intensity": 0.6,

            // Finally, blend the hillshade with the basemap
            "raster-opacity": 0.75
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // San Francisco (just an example)
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 45, // tilt the map so the relief is visible
    bearing: -17
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣ Optional UI – toggle hillshade on/off
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.addControl({
    onAdd: function () {
      const btn = document.createElement('button');
      btn.textContent = '🗻';
      btn.title = 'Toggle hillshade';
      btn.style.fontSize = '24px';
      btn.style.background = 'white';
      btn.style.border = 'none';
      btn.style.cursor = 'pointer';
      btn.onclick = () => {
        const visible = map.getLayoutProperty('hillshade', 'visibility');
        map.setLayoutProperty('hillshade', 'visibility',
          visible === 'none' ? 'visible' : 'none');
      };
      const container = document.createElement('div');
      container.style.position = 'absolute';
      container.style.top = '10px';
      container.style.right = '10px';
      container.appendChild(btn);
      return container;
    },
    onRemove: function () {}
  }, 'top-right');

</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What each part does

| Section | Explanation |
|---------|-------------|
| **DEM source** (`type: "raster-dem"`) | Supplies elevation data to the hillshade renderer. The public MapLibre DEM is 30 m SRTM; you can replace it with any Cloud‑Optimized GeoTIFF (COG) or XYZ tile set that follows the raster‑dem spec. |
| **`hillshade` layer** | The only layer that actually draws the relief. All the “softening” knobs live in its `paint` object. |
| **`hillshade-exaggeration`** | 1 = default. Values < 1 compress the slope‑derived contrast. 0.5‑0.7 is a sweet spot for most global DEMs. |
| **`hillshade-illumination-direction`** | Sun azimuth. Rotating it a little (e.g. 315° = NW) breaks up the repetitive north‑south striping you often see with the default 0°. |
| **`hillshade-shadow-color` / `hillshade-highlight-color`** | Use semi‑transparent greys instead of pure black/white. This lets the underlying basemap show through and reduces the “hard‑edge” feel. |
| **`hillshade-ambient-occlusion-intensity`** | Adds a subtle, global darkening in valleys (think “soft shadows”). Works well when the DEM is not super‑high‑resolution. |
| **`raster-opacity`** | Controls how much the hillshade dominates the visual hierarchy. 0.6‑0.8 usually feels natural. |
| **Map pitch & bearing** | Tilting the map (`pitch`) makes the relief visible. You can keep the map flat (`pitch: 0`) if you only need a subtle shading effect. |

---

## 3️⃣ Fine‑tuning tips for *your* data

1. **Know your DEM’s resolution**  
   - If you’re using a **30 m SRTM** DEM, `hillshade-exaggeration` ≈ 0.5‑0.6 works well.  
   - For **high‑resolution LiDAR** (≤ 5 m), you can push the exaggeration up to `0.9` or even `1.2` without looking “harsh”.  

2. **Play with the sun angle**  
   - `hillshade-illumination-direction` can be animated (e.g. a UI slider) to let users explore the terrain from different angles.  
   - `hillshade-illumination-anchor` defaults to `"viewport"` (sun moves with the map). If you want a fixed sun (like a real‑world time of day), set it to `"map"`.

3. **Combine with a raster‑basemap**  
   - If you want the hillshade *only* as a subtle texture, set the basemap’s `raster-saturation` or `raster-brightness-min/max` to mute colours, then raise the hillshade’s opacity a bit.  

4. **Add a “color ramp” overlay** (optional)  
   - For a more cartographic look, you can add a **terrain‑color** layer (type: `fill`) that uses a data‑driven style based on the DEM’s elevation (`["get", "ele"]`). Keep its opacity low (≈ 0.2) and blend it with the hillshade for a “shaded relief” effect.

   ```js
   map.addLayer({
     id: "terrain-color",
     type: "fill",
     source: "dem",
     paint: {
       "fill-color": [
         "interpolate",
         ["linear"],
         ["get", "ele"],
         0,   "#f2f0e6",
         500, "#d9d2b6",
         1500,"#b5a581",
         3000,"#8c7c5e"
       ],
       "fill-opacity": 0.25
     }
   });
   ```

5. **Performance tip**  
   - Hillshade is computed on the GPU, but the DEM tiles still need to be fetched. Keep `maxzoom` on the DEM source close to the highest zoom you actually display (e.g. `maxzoom: 12` for 30 m SRTM).  
   - If you notice “popping” of tiles when zooming, add a `source-layer` with a higher‑resolution DEM for the higher zoom levels.

---

## 4️⃣ Quick checklist to “fix a harsh hillshade”

| ✅ Done? | Action |
|--------|--------|
| **[ ]** | Lower `hillshade-exaggeration` to `0.5‑0.7`. |
| **[ ]** | Change `hillshade-illumination-direction` away from `0` (try `315`). |
| **[ ]** | Replace default black/white with semi‑transparent greys (`rgba(...,0.4‑0.5)`). |
| **[ ]** | Reduce `raster-opacity` to `0.6‑0.8`. |
| **[ ]** | Turn on `hillshade-ambient-occlusion-intensity` (`0.5‑0.8`). |
| **[ ]** | Verify DEM resolution matches your zoom range (adjust `maxzoom`). |
| **[ ]** | (Optional) Add a low‑opacity terrain‑color fill for extra depth. |
| **[ ]** | (Optional) Add UI sliders for `exaggeration` and `direction` to let users fine‑tune. |

