Will answer concisely but with details.

Below is a quick‑start guide that shows **which tile source you can use for OSM road + building data** and **how to hook it into a MapLibre style**.

---

## 1️⃣ Pick a tile provider

| Provider | Tile type | URL pattern (no API key) | Free tier / notes |
|----------|-----------|--------------------------|-------------------|
| **OpenMapTiles (free‑tier on tilehosting.com)** | Vector (`.pbf`) | `https://free.tilehosting.com/data/v3.json?key=YOUR_KEY` | 100 k tile‑requests / day, includes roads, buildings, landuse, water, etc. |
| **MapTiler (free‑tier)** | Vector (`.pbf`) | `https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/v3/tiles.json?key=YOUR_KEY` | 100 k requests / day, same layer set as OpenMapTiles. |
| **OpenStreetMap raster tiles** | Raster (`.png`) | `https://{a-c}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | Good for quick prototypes; no vector styling. |
| **Stadia Maps (OSM‑based raster)** | Raster (`.png`) | `https://tiles.stadiamaps.com/tiles/alidade_smooth/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | Free for low‑traffic sites, requires attribution. |

> **Why a vector source?**  
> With a vector source you can style *roads* and *buildings* exactly the way you want (line width, colour, 3‑D extrusion, etc.). Raster tiles are just images – you can only change opacity or blend modes.

---

## 2️⃣ Add the source to your **style JSON**

Below is a minimal style that pulls the OSM data from **OpenMapTiles** (the same structure works for MapTiler – just swap the URL).  
If you prefer the raster OSM tiles, see the “Raster alternative” section at the bottom.

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "name": "OSM Roads + Buildings (Vector)",
  "glyphs": "https://fonts.openmaptiles.org/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "sources": {
    "osm": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "https://free.tilehosting.com/data/v3.json?key=YOUR_KEY"
      /* For MapTiler use:
         "url": "https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/v3/tiles.json?key=YOUR_KEY"
      */
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    /* ---- 1️⃣ Buildings (fill) ---- */
    {
      "id": "building-fill",
      "type": "fill",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "building",
      "paint": {
        "fill-color": "#f0e6d2",
        "fill-outline-color": "#c2b9a6"
      }
    },

    /* ---- 2️⃣ Roads (line) ---- */
    {
      "id": "road-primary",
      "type": "line",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "transportation",
      "filter": ["==", "class", "primary"],
      "paint": {
        "line-color": "#ff6600",
        "line-width": [
          "interpolate",
          ["linear"],
          ["zoom"],
          5, 0.5,
          12, 2,
          18, 8
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "road-secondary",
      "type": "line",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "transportation",
      "filter": ["==", "class", "secondary"],
      "paint": {
        "line-color": "#ff9900",
        "line-width": [
          "interpolate",
          ["linear"],
          ["zoom"],
          5, 0.4,
          12, 1.5,
          18, 6
        ]
      }
    },

    /* ---- 3️⃣ Optional: road labels (symbol) ---- */
    {
      "id": "road-labels",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "transportation_name",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name"],
        "text-font": ["Open Sans Regular", "Arial Unicode MS Regular"],
        "text-size": 12,
        "symbol-placement": "line"
      },
      "paint": {
        "text-color": "#333",
        "text-halo-color": "#fff",
        "text-halo-width": 1
      }
    }
  ],

  "metadata": {
    "mapbox:autocomposite": false
  },

  "center": [0, 0],
  "zoom": 2,
  "bearing": 0,
  "pitch": 0
}
```

### What you need to change

| Placeholder | What to replace |
|-------------|-----------------|
| `YOUR_KEY` | Your free API key from **tilehosting.com** (or MapTiler). Sign‑up is instant. |
| `glyphs` URL | The OpenMapTiles glyph server works out‑of‑the‑box, but you can also host your own fonts. |
| `center`, `zoom` | Set to the initial view you want. |

---

## 3️⃣ Reference the source in **MapLibre GL JS** code

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// 1️⃣ Create the map
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div>
  style: 'path/to/your/style.json', // the JSON above (or a URL)
  center: [13.4, 52.5],           // Berlin, for example
  zoom: 12
});

// 2️⃣ (Optional) Add a raster fallback for low‑zoom levels
map.on('style.load', () => {
  // If you want a background raster basemap underneath the vector data:
  map.addSource('osm-raster', {
    type: 'raster',
    tiles: ['https://{a-c}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
    tileSize: 256,
    attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
  });
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'osm-raster-bg',
    type: 'raster',
    source: 'osm-raster',
    minzoom: 0,
    maxzoom: 5   // show raster only at low zooms
  });
});
```

That’s it – the map will now draw OSM **roads** (from the `transportation` layer) and **buildings** (from the `building` layer) using the vector tiles you referenced.

---

## 📦 Raster‑only alternative (if you don’t need styling)

If you just want a quick background and are happy with the default OSM look, you can skip the vector source entirely:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "osm-raster": {
      "type": "raster",
      "tiles": [
        "https://{a-c}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"
      ],
      "tileSize": 256,
      "attribution": "© OpenStreetMap contributors"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "osm-bg",
      "type": "raster",
      "source": "osm-raster"
    }
  ]
}
```

You can then add your own vector layers on top of this raster background if you later decide you need custom styling.

---

## ✅ Quick checklist

1. **Get an API key**  
   *Free Tilehosting*: https://free.tilehosting.com/ → “Get API key”.  
   *MapTiler*: https://cloud.maptiler.com/ → “Create API key”.

2. **Add the source** (`type: "vector"` and the `url` shown above) to your style JSON.

3. **Reference the correct `source-layer` names**  
   * `building` → building footprints.  
   * `transportation` → all road geometries (use `class` filter to pick primary, secondary, etc.).  
   * `transportation_name` → road name labels (optional).

4. **Add layers** that point to that source (`source: "osm"`). Use `filter` to isolate the road class you want.

5. **Include proper attribution** (OSM © OpenStreetMap contributors) – MapLibre will automatically show it if you copy the `attribution` field from the source definition.

---

### 🎉 You’re ready!

Drop the style JSON into your project, point MapLibre at it, and you’ll have a fully‑styled OSM road + building map. From there you can keep adding more layers (water, landuse, POIs, 3‑D building extrusion, etc.) – all using the same vector tile source. Happy mapping!
